  "content": "# Why Database Indexes Speed Up Reads but Slow Down Writes\n\n## The Core Idea\n\nAn index is a **separate data structure** (usually a B-tree or hash table) that maps column values to the physical locations of rows. It's like the index at the back of a textbook: instead of scanning every page to find a topic, you look it up alphabetically and jump straight to the right page.\n\nThat extra structure is what creates the trade-off.\n\n---\n\n## Why Reads Get Faster\n\nWithout an index, a query like:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'alice@example.com';\n```\n\nforces a **full table scan** — the database reads every row until it finds a match. If the table has 10 million rows, that's potentially 10 million row reads.\n\nWith an index on `email`, the database walks a B-tree (typically ~3–4 levels deep for millions of rows) and lands on the matching row in a handful of comparisons. This turns an **O(n)** operation into roughly **O(log n)**.\n\nIndexes also help with:\n- `ORDER BY` (data is already sorted in the index)\n- `JOIN` conditions (fast lookups on the join key)\n- `",
